Let $S$ be a finite vector space and let the episode updates on $D\subseteq E$ be $$G_e(v)=vL_e+b_e,$$ with $L_e\in\operatorname{End}_{\rm lin}(S)$ and $b_e\in S$ determined by $e$. If $R_{x,z}$ are the graph languages of their composites on $D^*$, then the graph language for the product of their linear parts, from $x$ to $y$, is $$\label{alg:linear-recovery-formula}
                  \bigcup_{b\in S}
                         \bigl(R_{x,y+b}\cap R_{0,b}\bigr).$$ A common height bound for the $R_{x,z}$ is preserved.

For $1\le j\le g$, put $c_j=(j+\tfrac12)G$. The main cuts of block $j$ are $$\label{schedule:main-cuts}
 k=t+c_j+aB,\qquad 0\le a<R.$$ At any such cut, all inner searches through index $j-1$, including their marker inspections, lie before $k$. The searches from index $j$ onward lie after $k$ even when performed at any of the hypothesized origins $k-c_j-a'B$, $0\le a'<R$, and with any width index $u$.*


*For $0\le j\le g$ and $0\le u<B$, the periodic cuts are $$\label{schedule:periodic-cut-formula}
 k=z_j(t)+H_u+L_{\rm lag}+aB,
 \qquad 0\le a<ndN_M.$$ The whole search for $f_j^u(t)$, with its inspections, lies before every one of these cuts. Every offset $k-t$ in either schedule is divisible by $B$.
